sePremium(components);\n};                                                     // ~28 mass\n```\n\nCombined mass ≈ **54**.\n\n## Step 3: Apply Rules\n\n- **Rule 1 (Tests Pass):** All green. ✓\n- **Rule 3 (No Duplication):** `baseTotalForItems` iterates `items` **twice** with `filter` (once for components, once negated for main items), then groups a subset. This is conceptual duplication of traversal. The block-vs-individual logic already lives in the grouping code.\n- **Rule 4 (Fewest Elements):** `componentsBasePremium` is a separate abstraction that exists only because components were pre-filtered. If we group **all** items by type once, the block rule (only for component types with exactly 3 alike) can be expressed uniformly, eliminating an entire function and both filter passes.\n\nKey insight: grouping main items by type is harmless — a group of 3 swords is not a component type, so no block applies; main items are still summed individually.\n\n## Step 4: Implement Refactoring"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"toolu_vrtx_01MDhhB1RzUYpGZAcWZSoExN","name":"edit","arguments":{"path":"src/claim-office.ts","oldText":"// A building block is offered only when there are EXACTLY BLOCK_SIZE alike\n// (same-type) components; otherwise every component is priced individually at\n// its own base premium. \"Alike\" means the same component type.\nconst componentsBasePremium = (components: Item[]): number =>\n  groupByType(components).reduce(\n    (total, group) =>\n      total +\n      (group.length === BLOCK_SIZE ? BLOCK_BASE_PREMIUM : sumBasePremiums(group)),\n    0,\n  );\n\nconst baseTotalForItems = (items: Item[]): number => {\n  const components = items.filter(isComponent);\n  const mainItems = items.filter((item) => !isComponent(item));\n  return sumBasePremiums(mainItems) + componentsBasePremium(components);\n};","newText":"// A building block is offered only when there are EXACTLY BLOCK_SIZE alike\n// (same-type) components; otherwise items are priced individually at their o