aseTotal + itemSurcharges + firstInsurance + PROCESSING_FEE,\n  );\n};\n```\n\n**Current `quotePremium` mass:**\n- Constants: `0` (in reduce) = 1\n- Bindings: `items`, `baseTotal`, `itemSurcharges`, `sum`, `item`, `firstInsurance` = 6\n- Invocations: `baseTotalForItems()`, `items.reduce()`, `itemSurcharge()`, `+`(sum+surcharge), `*`, `roundUpInFavorOfMHPCO()`, `+ + +`(3 adds) = 9 → mass 18\n- Conditionals: 0 = 0\n- Loops: 1 (reduce) = 5\n- Assignments: 0 = 0\n\n**Total `quotePremium` mass: 1 + 6 + 18 + 5 = 30**\n\n## Step 3: Apply Simple Design Rules\n\n**Rule 1 (Tests Pass)**: ✓ All 12 green.\n\n**Rule 2 (Reveals Intent)**: The `quotePremium` body reads well, but there's a subtle conceptual point. `baseTotal` is computed via `baseTotalForItems`, and `itemSurcharges` via an inline `reduce`. These are two separate item-traversal expressions with different styles (one delegated helper, one inline loop). Extracting the surcharge sum into a named helper — parallel to `baseTotalForItems` — would improve intent symmetry and consistency.\n\n**Rule 3 (No Duplication)**: There's conceptual duplication in the traversal style asymmetry noted above. Extracting `surchargeTotalForItems` mirrors `baseTotalForItems`, making the two policy components read symmetrically.\n\n**Rule 4 (Fewest Elements)**: Extraction adds one element but improves clarity — Rule 2 trumps here.\n\n## Step 4: Implement Refactoring\n\nI'll extract the inline surcharge reduction into a named helper, parallel to `baseTotalForItems`, so `quotePremium` reads as four clearly-named additive components."},{"type":"toolCall","id":"toolu_vrtx_01YPzuUskcZtaCwpSMSK8H5F","name":"edit","arguments":{"path":"src/claim-office.ts","edits":[{"oldText":"const itemSurcharge = (item: Item): number => {\n  const base = itemBasePremium(item);\n  return item.cursed ? base * CURSE_SURCHARGE_RATE : 0;\n};\n\nconst quotePremium = (items: Item[]): number => {\n  const baseTotal = baseTotalForItems(items);\n  const itemSurcharges = ite