ed through as a shared reference, so mutations stay localized to where they're needed. I'm defining a quote handler that sets up the initial capacity, a helper to calculate total damages, and starting on a claim handler that will compute the payout.\n\nExtracting claim handler... The dispatch pattern makes the code cleaner by separating concerns between quote and claim processing.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"All 24 tests pass. Now let me refactor.\n\n**Step 1: Naming Evaluation**\n- `runScenario`: executes a scenario's steps and returns results — name fits, keep it.\n- The inner `map` callback mixes quote handling and claim handling with cap mutation. This obscures intent. Extracting named handlers (`processQuote`, `processClaim`) will let `runScenario` read purely as a dispatch.\n\n**Step 2: Initial APP Mass of `runScenario`**\n```\nconst remainingCapByPolicy: number[] = [];   bindings: remainingCapByPolicy(1) + [] constant(1)\nconst results = scenario.steps.map(...)        binding results(1), invocation map(2)\n  if (step.op === \"quote\")                     conditional(4)\n    remainingCapByPolicy[index] = ...          assignment(6), invocations insuranceSum(2), *(2)\n    return { premium: quotePremium(...) }      invocation(2), index>0 conditional(4)\n  reduce(...)                                   invocation(2), - (2), binding damageTotal(1)\n  payout = roundDown(...)                       binding(1), invocation(2)\n  remainingCapByPolicy[step.policy] -= payout  assignment(6)\n```\nThe callback is a monolith doing two unrelated jobs. Rule 2 (reveals intent) drives extraction.\n\nLet me apply the refactoring:"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"toolu_vrtx_01TnQAK9y3GeFhCqzuy8mZRV","name":"edit","arguments":{"path":"/home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-24_15-55-54_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2-with-why-cleaned-pi_opus-4-8-no-thinking/src/claim-office.ts","edits":[{"oldText":"export const runScenario = (scenario: Scenario): ScenarioResult => {\n  const remainingCapByPolicy: number[] = [];\n  const results = scenario.steps.map((step, index): unknown => {\n    if (step.op === \"quote\") {\n      remainingCapByPolicy[index] = insuranceSum(step.items) * CAP_MULTIPLIER;\n      return {\n        premium: quotePremium(step.items, {\n          customer: scenario.customer,\n          isFollowUp: index > 0,\n        }),\n      };\n    }\n    const damageTotal = step.incident.damages.reduce(\n      (sum, damage) => sum + (damage.amount - DEDUCTIBLE),\n      0,\n    );\n    const payout = roundDownInFavorOfMHPCO(damageTotal);\n    remainingCapByPolicy[step.policy] -= payout;\n    return { payout, remainingCap: remainingCapByPolicy[step.policy] };\n  });\n  return { results };\n};","newText":"// A quote both prices the policy and records its claim cap (twice the\n// insurance sum) keyed by the step's position, so later claims can draw down\n// against it.\nconst processQuote = (\n  step: QuoteStep,\n  index: number,\n  scenario: Scenario,\n  remainingCapByPolicy: number[],\n): unknown => {\n  remainingCapByPolicy[index] = insuranceSum(step.items) * CAP_MULTIPLIER;\n  return {\n    premium: quotePremium(step.items, {\n      customer: scenario.customer,\n      isFollowUp: index > 0,\n    }),\n  };\n};\n\n// Each damage is reimbursed at its amount less the deductible; the payout is\n// their sum, rounded down in MHPCO's favor.\nconst claimPayout = (incident: Incident): number =>\n  roundDownInFavorOfMHPCO(\n    incident.damages.reduce(\n      (sum, damage) => sum + (damage.amount - DEDUCTIBLE),\n      0,\n    ),\n  );\n\n// A claim draws its payout down from the referenced policy's remaining cap and\n// reports both figures.\nconst processClaim = (\n  step: ClaimStep,\n  remainingCapByPolicy: number[],\n): unknown => {\n  const payout = claimPayout(step.incident);\n  remainingCapByPolicy[step.policy] -= payout;\n  return { payout, remainingCap: remainingCapByPolicy[step.policy] };\n};\n\nexport const 