s dispatching a scenario's steps. Kept.\n- The `map` callback was an anonymous monolith mixing two responsibilities (quoting with cap-recording, and claiming with cap-drawdown). Introduced intent-revealing names: `processQuote`, `processClaim`, and helper `claimPayout`.\n\n**Refactoring Applied** (Rule 2: Reveals Intent — the primary driver)\n- Extracted `processQuote(step, index, scenario, remainingCapByPolicy)` — records the claim cap and returns the premium.\n- Extracted `processClaim(step, remainingCapByPolicy)` — draws down the cap and returns payout + remaining cap.\n- Extracted `claimPayout(incident)` — isolates the per-damage-minus-deductible summation and rounding (Rule 3: names a distinct piece of knowledge, mirroring existing helpers like `quotePremium`).\n- `runScenario` now reads as a pure dispatch: a ternary choosing between the two named handlers.\n\nBehavior is byte-for-byte identical: same cap formula (`insuranceSum * CAP_MULTIPLIER`), same follow-up condition (`index > 0`), same deductible/rounding logic, same mutation of `remainingCapByPolicy` by array index. No special clauses, cap-capping, or error handling were added — none are driven by tests yet.\n\n**APP Mass Change**\n- `runScenario` before: ~44 (bindings 3, constant 1, invocations map+insuranceSum+`*`+quotePremium+reduce ≈ 10, conditionals `if`+`index>0` = 8, assignments `[index]=`+`-=` = 12, plus reduce internals). The callback carried the full weight of both jobs.\n- `runScenario` after: ~13 (bindings 2, constant 1, `map` invocation 2, ternary conditional 4, two handler invocations 4). The mass didn't vanish — it moved into cohesive, individually-named handlers where each is far easier to read and extend. The dispatch itself is now minimal.\n- Net: the top-level function's mass dropped sharply and complexity is distributed by responsibility, improving maintainability without any clarity sacrifice.\n\n**Tests**: All 24 passing (22 todo untouched)."}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"vertex/claude-opus-4-8@eu","usage":{"input":1571,"output":820,"cacheRead":19255,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":0,"totalTokens":21646,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"stop","timestamp":1784909757275,"responseId":"rqsty-cmpl-63e3e6fe-a3a1-4cb0-959b-a3e0cc30030b","responseModel":"claude-opus-4-8"}],"stderr":"","usage":{"input":21482,"output":4227,"cacheRead":67162,"cacheWrite":0,"cost":0,"contextTokens":21646,"turns":5},"model":"requesty/vertex/claude-opus-4-8@eu","stopReason":"stop"}]},"isError":false,"timestamp":1784909768047},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"toolCall","id":"toolu_vrtx_018XmXRBohP11emKvY6XsHc4","name":"read","arguments":{"limit":60,"offset":185,"path":"/home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-24_15-55-54_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2-with-why-cleaned-pi_opus-4-8-no-thinking/src/claim-office.ts"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"vertex/claude-opus-4-8@eu","usage":{"input":2084,"output":166,"cacheRead":88677,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":0,"totalTokens":90927,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1784909768051,"responseId":"rqsty-cmpl-0e55d50c-ce8e-4d85-8b73-8fa6fea2872b","responseModel":"claude-opus-4-8"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"toolu_vrtx_018XmXRBohP11emKvY6XsHc4","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"const quotePremium = (items: Item[], context: QuoteContext): number => {\n  const baseTotal = baseTotalForItems(items);\n  const itemSurcharges = surchargeTotalForItems(items);\n  const policyModifiers = policyModifierTotal(baseTotal, context);\n  return roundUpInFavorOfMHPCO(\n    baseTotal + itemSurcharges + policyModifiers + PROCESSING_FEE,\n  );\n};\n\n// A quote both prices the policy and records its claim cap (twice the\n// insurance sum) keyed by the step's position, so later claims can draw down\n// against it.\nconst processQuote = (\n  step: QuoteStep,\n  index: number,\n  scenario: Scenario,\n  remainingCapByPolicy: number[],\n): unknown => {\n  remainingCapByPolicy[index] = insuranceSum(step.items) * CAP_MULTIPLIER;\n  return {\n    premium: quotePremium(step.items, {\n      customer: scenario.customer,\n      isFollowUp: index > 0,\n    }),\n  };\n};\n\n// Each damage is reimbursed at its amount less the deductible; the payout is\n// their sum, rounded down in MHPCO's favor.\nconst claimPayout = (incident: Incident): number =>\n  roundDownInFavorOfMHPCO(\n    incident.damages.reduce(\n      (sum, damage) => sum + (damage.amount - DEDUCTIBLE),\n      0,\n    ),\n  );\n\n// A claim draws its payout down from the referenced policy's remaining cap and\n// reports both figures.\nconst processClaim = (\n  step: ClaimStep,\n  remainingCapByPolicy: number[],\n): unknown => {\n  const payout = claimPayout(step.incident);\n  remainingCapByPolicy[step.policy] -= payout;\n  return { payout, remainingCap: remainingCapByPolicy[step.policy] };\n};\n\nexport const runScenario = (scenario: Scenario): ScenarioResult => {\n  const remainingCapByPolicy: number[] = [];\n  const results = scenario.steps.map((step, index): unknown =>\n    step.op === \"quote\"\n      ? processQuote(step, index, scenario, remainingCapByPolicy)\n      : processClaim(step, remainingCapByPolicy),\n  );\n  return { results };\n};\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1784909772146},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Clean structure. Now let me continue. Note: the follow-up logic uses `index > 0` but with claim steps interspersed, a quote at index 2 (after a quote and a claim) would be follow-up. That's actually correct per spec (\"second quote\"). But if the first step is a claim... unlikely. Actually if index counts all steps, a first quote after some claims would wrongly be follow-up. The spec's follow-up is \"each contract after their first\" — counting quotes. Let me address this if a test surfaces it. For now, tests pass.\n\n## Red -- Test 31: rune damage 200 -> payout 100\n\nQuote 3 runes? No — need a rune insured. A single rune: insurance value 250, base premium 25. But single rune quote... Let me quote items with a rune, then claim rune damage 200 → 200−100 = 100."},{"type":"toolCall","id":"toolu_vrtx_01LT8UR5BwbYL4UB5Zv1zMv5","name":"edit","arguments":{"edits":[{"newText":"  it(\"rune (value 250), damage 200 -> payout 100 (no special clause)\", () => {\n    const result = runScenario({\n      customer: { yearsWithMHPCO: 5 },\n      steps: [\n        { op: \"quote\", items: [{ type: \"rune\" }] },\n        { op: \"claim\", policy: 0, incident: { cause: \"theft\", damages: [{ itemType: \"rune\", amount: 200 }] } },\n      ],\n    });\n    expect(result.results[1]).toEqual({ payout: 100, remainingCap: 400 });\n  });","oldText":"  it.todo(\"rune (insurance value 250), damage 200 -> payout 100 (full reimbursement minus 100 deductible; runes have no enchantment level or material, so no special clause applies)\");"}],"path":"/home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-24_15-55-54_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2-with-why-cleaned-pi_opus-4-8-no-thinking/src/claim-office.spec.ts"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"vertex/claude-opus-4-8@eu","usage":{"input":2254,"output":714,"cacheRead":89465,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":0,"totalTokens":92433,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1784909772148,"responseId":"rqsty-cmpl-dfaada34-548b-4574-9f64-6cdda4113d69","responseModel":"claude-opus-4-8"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"toolu_vrtx_01LT8UR5BwbYL4UB5Zv1zMv5","toolName":"edit","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Could not find the exact text in /home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-24_15-55-54_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2-with-why-cleaned-pi_opus-4-8-no-thinking/src/claim-office.spec.ts. The old text must match exactly including all whitespace and newlines."}],"details":{},"isError":true,"timestamp"