les that references follow-up logic that seems outdated.\n\nI think the cleanest improvement would be extracting the negative-amount validation into its own named helper function to make the intent clearer and keep the concerns separated.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"All 45 tests pass and tsc is clean. Let me analyze the code for refactoring opportunities.\n\nThe implementation is already quite clean. The clearest issue I see is in `validateClaim`, which mixes two distinct validation concerns (non-negative amounts and count limits) in one function, and there's an orphaned comment above `FOLLOW_UP_DISCOUNT_RATE` that actually describes `POLICY_MODIFIER_RULES`.\n\n**Naming Evaluation (FIRST PRIORITY)**:\n- `validateClaim` — good name, but the function bundles two unrelated checks inline. Extracting each into an intention-revealing predicate improves Rule 2 (reveals intent).\n- The orphaned comment block is a clarity defect: it describes `POLICY_MODIFIER_RULES` but sits above unrelated declarations.\n\n**Current Mass of `validateClaim`**:\n- bindings: `incident, policy, damage, insuredCountByType, damagedCountByType, type, damaged` ≈ 7\n- invocations: several (`countByType`×2, `.get`×3, `Error`×2, arrow calls) ≈ ~12 → 24\n- conditionals: 3 (`if amount<0`, `if damaged>`, two `?? 0`) ≈ 3–4 → ~16\n- loops: 3 (negative loop, damaged loop, plus inner countByType hidden) → 2 visible = 10\n- constants: literals → ~3\n\nLet me apply the refactoring: extract the two validation concerns into named helpers and fix the misplaced comment."},{"type":"toolCall","id":"toolu_vrtx_01G7DPxnhnErw46CxoA8M5QV","name":"edit","arguments":{"path":"/home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-24_15-55-54_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2-with-why-cleaned-pi_opus-4-8-no-thinking/src/claim-office.ts","edits":[{"oldText":"// Policy-wide modifiers are rates applied to the whole policy's base total,\n// gated by a condition on the customer. A positive rate is a surcharge, a\n// negative rate a discount. Modelling them as a list (mirroring\n// SURCHARGE_RULES) keeps every policy-level rule in one place and makes the\n// total an additive sum of the applicable rates.\nconst FOLLOW_UP_DISCOUNT_RATE = 0.15;\n\n// Everything needed to evaluate policy-wide modifiers for one quote: the\n// customer plus whether this is a follow-up (any quote after the customer's\n// first in the scenario).\ninterface QuoteContext {\n  customer: Customer;\n  isFollowUp: boolean;\n}\n\nconst POLICY_MODIFIER_RULES: {","newText":"const FOLLOW_UP_DISCOUNT_RATE = 0.15;\n\n// Everything needed to evaluate policy-wide modifiers for one quote: the\n// customer plus whether this is a follow-up (any quote after the customer's\n// first in the scenario).\ninterface QuoteContext {\n  customer: Customer;\n  isFollowUp: boolean;\n}\n\n// Policy-wide modifiers are rates applied to the whole policy's base total,\n// gated by a condition on the quote context. A positive rate is a surcharge, a\n// negative rate a discount. Modelling them as a list (mirroring\n// SURCHARGE_RULES) keeps every policy-level rule in one place and makes the\n// total an additive sum of the applicable rates.\nconst POLICY_MODIFIER_RULES: {"},{"oldText":"// A claim is only valid if, for every item type, it does not report more\n// damages than the policy actually covers of that type. Each damage entry is a\n// separate event against a distinct insured item.\nconst validateClaim = (incident: Incident, policy: Policy): void => {\n  for (const damage of incident.damages) {\n    if (damage.amount < 0) {\n      throw new Error(`damage amount ${damage.amount} must not be negative`);\n    }\n  }\n  const insuredCountByType = countByType(policy.items, (item) => item.type);\n  const damagedCountByType = countByType(\n    incident.damages,\n    (damage) => damage.itemType,\n  );\n  for (const [type, damaged] of damagedCountByType) {\n    if (damaged > (insuredCountByType.get(type) ?? 0)) {\n      throw new Error(\n        `claim reports ${damaged} damage(s) of type \"${type}\" but the policy covers ${insuredCountByType.get(type) ?? 0}`,\n      );\n    }\n  }\n};","newText":"// A damage amount is money owed to the customer; a negative amount is nonsense\n// and rejects the claim.\nconst validateDamageAmounts = (incident: Incident): void => {\n  for (const damage of incident.damages) {\n    if (damage.amount < 0) {\n      throw new Error(`damage amount ${damage.amount} must not be negative`);\n    }\n  }\n};\n\n// For every item type, a claim must not report more damages than the policy\n// actually covers of that type. Each damage entry is a separate event against a\n// distinct insured item.\nconst validateDamageCounts = (incident: Incident, policy: Policy): void => {\n  const insuredCountByType = countByType(policy.items, (item) => item.type);\n  const damagedCountByType = countByType(\n    incident.damages,\n    (damage) => damage.itemType,\n  );\n  for (const [type, damaged] of damagedCountByType) {\n    const insured = insuredCountByType.get(type) ?? 0;\n    if (damaged > insured) {\n      throw new Error(\n        `claim reports ${damaged} damage(s) of type \"${type}\" but the policy covers ${insured}`,\n      );\n    }\n  }\n};\n\n// A claim is valid only when every damage amount is sane and no item type is\n// over-claimed against the policy's coverage.\nconst validateClaim = (incident: Incident, policy: Policy): void => {\n  validateDamageAmounts(incident);\n  validateDamageCounts(incident, policy);\n};"}]}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"vertex/claude-opus-4-8@eu","usage":{"input":5776,"output":2695,"cacheRead":10003,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":0,"totalTokens":18474,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1784910418573,"responseId":"rqsty-cmpl-5ea159c8-d255-4c2b-902c-7f15f4dba4dc","responseModel":"claude-opus-4-8"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"toolu_vrtx_01G